/-!
# The fibers on which no perfect cuboid lies

Every perfect cuboid lies on a fiber `(m, n)` of Peschmann's master-tuple parametrization
(`IsEulerBrick.exists_fiber`): two of its edges `X, Z` satisfy `(m² − n²) X = 2mn Z` for coprime
`m > n > 0` of opposite parity. This file collects the 91 fibers that the descent of
`LeanPlayground.PerfectCuboid.Descent` has ruled out unconditionally, in `FiberTwoOne.lean`,
`FiberDescent.lean` and `Fibers/F_<p>_<q>.lean`, into one statement:

* `provedFibers` — the list of the 91 pairs `(m, n)`;
* `IsPerfectCuboid.not_onFiber_of_mem` — a perfect cuboid has no two edges `X, Z` with
  `(m² − n²) X = 2mn Z` for `(m, n)` in the list;
* `IsPerfectCuboid.exists_fiber_not_mem` — every perfect cuboid lies on a fiber outside the list.

With `U = m² − n²`, `V = 2mn`, `W = m² + n²`, such a cuboid gives a solution of the descent for
each of the pairs `(V, W)`, `(U, W)` and `(U, V)` (`IsPerfectCuboid.not_onFiber_of_pair` in
`LeanPlayground.PerfectCuboid.FiberPairs`), so one pair whose descent closes is enough.

Through the pair `(V, W)` (curve `y² = x (x + V²)(x + W²)`), 41 fibers, in terms of edge ratios
`3 : 4`, `5 : 12`, `15 : 8`, `7 : 24`, `35 : 12`, `45 : 28`, `13 : 84`, `63 : 16`, `77 : 36`,
`65 : 72`, `17 : 144`, `117 : 44`, `85 : 132`, `143 : 24`, `119 : 120`, `165 : 52`, `221 : 60`,
`247 : 96`, `135 : 352`, `87 : 416`, `285 : 68`, `273 : 136`, `203 : 396`, `155 : 468`, `345 : 152`,
`325 : 228`, `37 : 684`, `377 : 336`, `475 : 132`, `403 : 396`, `429 : 460`, `333 : 644`,
`273 : 736`, `527 : 336`, `621 : 100`, `561 : 400`, `675 : 52`, `667 : 156`, `665 : 432`,
`783 : 56`, `1363 : 684`.

Through the pair `(U, W)` (curve `y² = x (x + U²)(x + W²)`), 21 more: `21 : 20`, `33 : 56`,
`99 : 20`, `91 : 60`, `51 : 140`, `57 : 176`, `187 : 84`, `27 : 364`, `207 : 224`, `323 : 36`,
`299 : 180`, `35 : 612`, `297 : 304`, `261 : 380`, `437 : 84`, `459 : 220`, `259 : 660`,
`513 : 184`, `493 : 276`, `651 : 260`, `775 : 168`.

Through the pair `(U, V)` (curve `y² = x (x + U²)(x + V²)`, where `V² − U²` is not a square and the
descent runs with `D = |V² − U²|`, see `Descent.not_isSolD`), 24 more: `9 : 40`, `105 : 88`,
`15 : 112`, `133 : 156`, `19 : 180`, `21 : 220`, `115 : 252`, `255 : 32`, `175 : 288`, `75 : 308`,
`319 : 360`, `399 : 40`, `435 : 308`, `279 : 440`, `465 : 368`, `231 : 520`, `455 : 528`,
`165 : 532`, `385 : 552`, `589 : 300`, `595 : 468`, `627 : 364`, `629 : 540`, `533 : 756`.

Through the curve `E″` of `LeanPlayground.PerfectCuboid.Kubert`, i.e. the pair `(2UV, W²)`
(`IsPerfectCuboid.not_onFiber_of_isogeny`), 2 more: `357 : 76` and `817 : 744`, the fibers
`(19, 2)` and `(31, 12)`. Their descents use the class lemma of
`LeanPlayground.PerfectCuboid.DescentClass`, which kills whole groups of cases at once.

Through the curve `E″` itself, i.e. the pair `(U², V²)` (`IsPerfectCuboid.not_onFiber_of_sq`),
3 more: `171 : 140`, `851 : 420` and `77 : 2964`, the fibers `(14, 5)`, `(30, 7)` and
`(39, 38)`. This pair has the solutions `M² = pq e²` of the point `T₄` of order `4`, and the
descent shows that every solution is of this kind (`Descent.sq_eq_of_isSol_of_casesOKT`); the
cases in the class of a point of order `8` use family TR of
`LeanPlayground.PerfectCuboid.DescentTR`.

The descent can only work on a pair whose curve has rank `0` and `Ш[2] = 0`. By PARI/GP, among the
`331` fibers with `m ≤ 40`, `70` have a usable pair `(V, W)`, `88` a usable pair `(U, W)` and `98` a
usable pair `(U, V)`; `179` have at least one. The list has every fiber with a usable pair `(V, W)`
or `(U, W)` and `W ≤ 829` except `(22, 17)` (`W = 773` is prime and is needed as a modulus, and a
residue check modulo a prime that size costs minutes and gigabytes of kernel memory), and every
fiber with a usable pair `(U, V)` and `max(U, V) ≤ 800`. The fibers `(26, 9)` and `(27, 10)`, out of
reach through `(V, W)` and `(U, W)` for that reason, go through `(U, V)` with moduli at most `41`.
-/

